WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.940
Film Courage: Let’s say I’m a screenwriter
and I email you and need my script fixed a.s.a.p.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.730
and we agree to work together, what are the
first things that you’re looking at?

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:13.519
Jill Chamberlain, Author/Screenwriter: I’m
kind of simultaneously looking at both the

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:15.889
macro and micro level.

00:00:15.889 --> 00:00:26.390
So as I’m going ultimately the story is
of utmost importance but also as I’m reading

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:30.950
on the page noting things so I mark things
up as I go.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:37.430
So I may already have questions or suggestions
that have to do with minor kind of things

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:44.130
where I could think of a better phrasing or
your action description isn’t clear enough

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:48.810
especially those first 10 pages which are
super important where I am having a very hard

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.670
time slogging through those.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:57.980
That’s a really bad sign so I might even
spend more time trying to point that out.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:03.140
So I’m looking at the micro level but I’m
also sitting back and looking at the macro

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.140
level.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:09.249
How does this story work and I often don’t
know the answer to that until I get to the

00:01:09.249 --> 00:01:10.249
very end.

00:01:10.249 --> 00:01:12.990
I’m kind of doing two things at once.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:18.549
Film Courage: And then at what point do you
send them notes?

00:01:18.549 --> 00:01:21.259
Do they have to make changes for you to review?

00:01:21.259 --> 00:01:24.130
Or it’s up to them whether they want to
make those changes?

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:30.990
Jill: What I do is I meet with the writer
either person or via Skype and I have their

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.759
script marked up but I don’t do written
notes.

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:37.460
I don’t do a written report unless you want
to pay for that separately which I don’t

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:38.460
recommend.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.649
I don’t think it’s a very efficient use
of your money.

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:44.409
I’ll happily do it if you want to pay me
by the hour to sit and type up a report.

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:47.639
I don’t think it’s a very time efficient
use of your money.

00:01:47.639 --> 00:01:53.130
I think it’s much more efficient if we…so
with each of my consultations it includes

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:57.479
60-90 minutes where we talk either in person
or via Skype.

00:01:57.479 --> 00:02:03.259
So we go through on the page pointing out
things but also talking story.

00:02:03.259 --> 00:02:11.380
It’s not just that it’s time inefficient
for me to type up a report is to do that is

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:12.810
not a two-way thing.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:20.160
So it’s more efficient for me to start asking
questions and try to find out if something

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.040
is not working, try to find out what it was
that you’re trying to do.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:27.790
Versus doing some kind of detached cover report.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:32.680
That’s just not a very useful tool for a
writer.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.180
As a writer you want to know just how to make
this better.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:39.390
So for the way to make this better is to talk
about it, for us to talk about it, for us

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:42.680
to say “If that’s what we’re trying
to do, I totally did not get that this was

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.159
the character’s flaw.

00:02:44.159 --> 00:02:47.620
You need to look at this character’s first
scene and see if you can adjust and that’s

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:48.620
the flaw.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:50.400
If that’s the flaw they should be saying
this in the first scene.”

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.140
We want to look at specifics.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:56.109
So I’m really kind of script doctoring and
trying to help.

00:02:56.109 --> 00:03:02.170
I’m not just giving you a passive report
that tells you thumbs up or thumbs down.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:04.780
It’s not very useful.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:08.189
Who cares if I think it’s thumbs up or thumbs
down.

00:03:08.189 --> 00:03:11.959
My job is to make your script as good as it
can possibly be.

00:03:11.959 --> 00:03:13.519
So I like to have that two-way conversation.

00:03:13.519 --> 00:03:17.859
So we can talk about “Oh, that’s what
you’re trying to do if…I did not get that

00:03:17.859 --> 00:03:19.190
at all.

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:23.110
You need to bring it up in moments like this.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:26.030
I want to hear the character’s flaws, that
they are jealous.”

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:29.959
They should be saying that in this moment
or they should try doing this.

00:03:29.959 --> 00:03:33.109
So there is a two-way conversation that happens
there.

00:03:33.109 --> 00:03:39.129
Film Courage: Are most people receptive to
when you want to make changes or when you

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:40.129
suggest them?

00:03:40.129 --> 00:03:43.569
Jill: I would say 97% of the time, right?

00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:50.920
97% of the time they are and that’s why
I generally love my job.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:57.130
Is that people pay me to tell them what works
and what doesn’t work in their script.

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:58.659
And they say “Oh my gosh, thank you!

00:03:58.659 --> 00:04:00.239
That’s such a good suggestion.”

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:01.750
And they love it and they go with it.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:06.090
And then there’s the three percent where
I don’t know if they think I’m run a studio

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:09.700
or something or I’m some sort of gatekeeper
because I’m not.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:17.000
They are occasionally unhappy that I…and
this is not a productive thing.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.049
You’re not trying to convince me, why are
you trying to convince me that your script

00:04:21.049 --> 00:04:24.830
doesn’t work when I telling you it doesn’t
work.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:30.169
I’m trying to help you so you can make it
work.

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:31.550
So occasionally you find someone who is resistant.

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:34.960
Film Courage: That’s funny because that
prompted my memory for something.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.729
I was watching this old interview with [documentary
filmmaker] Errol Morris and he said “Most

00:04:38.729 --> 00:04:41.240
people don’t see themselves are they are.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.810
And other people are better gauges at who
they are.”

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:47.030
You know that cliche saying “I know you
better than you know yourself.”

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:50.600
I’ve always hated that expression but he
said that is actually kind of true especially

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:51.810
in the retelling of his things.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.479
Do you think this is true with writers?

00:04:53.479 --> 00:04:55.710
Jill: Do you mean as far as their ability
or…?

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:56.720
Film Courage: Yes.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:01.500
Jill: You know I’m actually surprised that
for the most part (and the reason that 97%

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:08.560
of them are receptive) is that deep down they
do know and that is why most of them go well

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:13.560
is that they are not generally shocked at
what I’m going to say.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:18.580
They might be disappointed, they might have
hoped that they tried this thing and that

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.770
it worked but they’re not shocked.

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.770
They’re kind of like “Yeah, I kind of
suspected it.”

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:29.681
And they are trying and that is part of the
point some of the things work and I’ll tell

00:05:29.681 --> 00:05:34.569
you that and it will be pleasantly surprised
and then other ones aren’t and it’s going

00:05:34.569 --> 00:05:36.110
to confirm what you maybe suspected.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:41.310
But that’s the point of getting the draft
done and on paper and out of your head so

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:45.470
that someone…or have a professional read
it so you can find out what works and what

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:46.470
doesn’t.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:49.800
And some things you will surprise yourself
and do better than you thought and some things

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:51.010
not as well.

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:53.160
So for the most part people aren’t so surprised.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:06:03.580
I think those are the 3% are the ones who
have a disconnect in their head about what

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:05.950
it is and how it’s working.

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:08.990
97% of people don’t.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:10.949
They hope I love every single word.

00:06:10.949 --> 00:06:14.740
But when I point out something they are like
“Yeah, I kind of knew that wasn’t working.”

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:18.229
So it resonates with them.

00:06:18.229 --> 00:06:22.880
Film Courage: Do you think writers are very
self aware or they’ve been critiqued so

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:26.819
much either through school or classes that
they’ve taken.

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:31.170
That they just kind of know “Yeah, I know
I’m not using active voice here.

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:32.170
I’m using passive voice.”

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:33.170
I don’t know?

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:34.170
What do you think it is?

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:35.250
Jill: At varies at their level.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:38.160
It varies at how often they’ve been critiqued.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:39.660
It varies at the kind of critiques.

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:48.729
I see all different levels where people are
quite self aware and are quite used to criticism

00:06:48.729 --> 00:06:53.360
and are not going to be defensive and they
know the things and they will say it right

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:56.180
out when I point out a weakness.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:00.849
They will be like “Yes, I’m not great
at doing action sequences.”

00:07:00.849 --> 00:07:05.429
And there are others who are not as self-aware,
it really depends.

